---
/**
 * Home, EN. Twin of `src/pages/index.astro` — see it for why both were rewritten
 * on 2026-08-11: measured on `dist/`, the home pages carried 115 (FR) and 106
 * (EN) words against 1,191 on a service hub, and passed almost nothing to the
 * money pages despite receiving the most authority. No gate saw it: link-audit
 * only enforces word and link thresholds on money pages, and home is not one.
 *
 * Rule 6: this is a translation of the ARGUMENT, not of the sentences. English
 * search phrasing differs — "french drain", "foundation crack", "weeping tile"
 * — and the copy leads with those rather than transliterating the French.
 */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pathFor } from '@lib/routes.ts';
import { dict } from '@lib/i18n.ts';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@lib/schema.ts';
import { resolveImage } from '@lib/images.ts';
import { cities, services as serviceData } from '@lib/data.ts';
import type { ServiceKey } from '@data/slugs.ts';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const ui = dict(locale);
const ref = { type: 'home' } as const;

const written = new Set(
  (await getCollection('services', (e) => e.data.locale === locale)).map((e) => e.data.service),
);

const ENTRIES: { key: ServiceKey; lead: string }[] = [
  { key: 'infiltration', lead: "Water is getting into the basement and you cannot tell where from yet." },
  { key: 'fissure', lead: "A crack has opened in the foundation wall, or it is widening." },
  { key: 'drain', lead: "The drain is as old as the house and may no longer carry water." },
  { key: 'ocre', lead: "An orange deposit is clogging the drain — a ground condition, not bad workmanship." },
  { key: 'impermeabilisation', lead: "The buried wall has lost its membrane, or never had one." },
  { key: 'inspection', lead: "Nobody has actually looked inside yet, and that decides everything else." },
];
const entries = ENTRIES.filter((e) => written.has(e.key)).map((e) => ({
  ...e,
  name: serviceData[locale][e.key].name,
}));

const sectorEntries = await getCollection('sectors', (e) => e.data.locale === locale);
const featured = ['la-cite-limoilou', 'beauport', 'charlesbourg', 'levis', 'sainte-foy-sillery-cap-rouge', 'les-rivieres']
  .filter((s) => sectorEntries.some((e) => e.data.sector === s))
  .map((s) => ({ slug: s, name: cities[s]!.name[locale] }));

const blogAll = await getCollection('blog');
const twins = new Set(blogAll.filter((e) => e.data.locale === 'fr').map((e) => e.data.translationKey));
const posts = blogAll
  .filter((e) => e.data.locale === 'en' && twins.has(e.data.translationKey))
  .map((e) => ({
    h1: e.data.h1,
    slugs: {
      en: e.data.slug,
      fr: blogAll.find((x) => x.data.locale === 'fr' && x.data.translationKey === e.data.translationKey)!.data.slug,
    },
  }));
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  ref={ref}
  title="Foundation Repair in Quebec City — Solage Capitale"
  description="Foundation cracks, french drains, iron ochre and basement water infiltration: what actually happens under a house in the Quebec City region."
  schema={[breadcrumbSchema(locale, [{ name: 'Home', ref }])]}
>
  <section class="contenant bloc-page">
    <div class="mesure">
      <h1>Foundations, french drains and wet basements in the Quebec City region</h1>

      <p class="chapeau">
        A crack in the foundation wall, water coming in as the snow melts, a drain that no longer
        carries: around Quebec City these problems almost always trace back to the same three
        causes — water, frost, and the ground the house sits on.
      </p>

      <figure class="hero-photo">
        <Image
          src={resolveImage('limoilou')}
          alt="Residential street in Limoilou under snow, lined with brick duplexes whose basements sit half below grade."
          widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
          sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 42rem, 100vw"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </figure>

      <p>
        This site explains what happens under a house here, what Quebec regulation entitles you to
        insist on, and what a licensed contractor does to put it right. It will never tell you how
        to work on a foundation yourself: a badly run excavation or injection injures people and
        destroys buildings.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>
        If you are not yet sure what you are dealing with, start from the symptom. It is the
        fastest way to the right page.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          entries.map((e) => (
            <li>
              <a href={pathFor({ type: 'service', service: e.key }, locale)}>{e.name}</a>
              {" — "}
              {e.lead}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        The full list is on
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'serviceIndex' }, locale)}>foundation services</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the Quebec City region specifically</h2>
      <p>
        The housing stock here is old, and it is old in ways worth knowing. A good share of the
        homes in the Quebec City Metropolitan Community went up before french drains — weeping
        tile — were common practice, so some never had one at all. Another share sits on
        <strong>clay tile drains</strong> laid between the 1950s and the 1970s, now reaching the
        end of their life just about everywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two regional factors compound it: a <strong>freeze-thaw</strong> cycle that works the soil
        against the buried wall every winter, and <strong>iron ochre</strong>, a deposit that can
        render a drainage system completely inoperative — including a brand-new drain, if the risk
        was never assessed before it went in.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why every area has its own page rather than one generic text: the era a house was
        built in changes the diagnosis.
      </p>

      <h2>Your area</h2>
      <ul class="liens-liste">
        {
          featured.map((s) => (
            <li><a href={pathFor({ type: 'sector', sector: s.slug }, locale)}>{s.name}</a></li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        All 51 areas covered, from the Quebec City boroughs to Lévis and the outer ring, are listed
        on <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'serviceArea' }, locale)}>the service area
        page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What you can insist on before signing</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part almost nobody writes, and it is public and checkable. A Régie du bâtiment
        du Québec licence can be looked up in the public register, a quote can be required to name
        the standard it works to, and the new home warranty plan covers certain defects for five
        years.
      </p>
      <ul class="liens-liste">
        {
          posts.map((p) => (
            <li><a href={pathFor({ type: 'blogPost', slug: p.slugs }, locale)}>{p.h1}</a></li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>How this service works</h2>
      <p>
        Solage Capitale is an information and referral service. When you request a quote, it goes
        to an <strong>independent company</strong> holding its own RBQ licence, which calls you
        back, visits and prices the work. It is free, carries no obligation, and you remain free to
        seek other quotes — which is in fact recommended. The detail is on
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'about' }, locale)}>about the service</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="cta-fin">
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>{ui('cta.quote')}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-photo {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }
  .hero-photo img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
</style>
